## ===========================================================================
## THE AERIAL RUNWAY — the two-turn slide and the hidden landing
##
## The slide down the anchor cable (`story.ni:3700-3830`, the source's own
## "Aerial Runway" chapter), reshaped by the change document's escape-disguise
## amendments (2026-08-23): the slide takes TWO turns, and the mid-slide text
## reveals the open spot behind the fruit stall — let go there and Jack drops
## in unseen; ride it out and she lands in front of the stall with Gentry's
## knocking-over-shoppers ending, seen.
##
## The machine, in turns:
##
##   1. `slide down northeast cable` (Top of the Post, cloak required) moves Jack to
##      On the Wire and starts the cables' one-turn `sliding` clock. The
##      turn's text is Gentry's first half; the room's own description is the
##      mid-slide reveal.
##   2. `let go` on the mid-slide turn drops her into Behind Fruit Stall —
##      the ONLY way in (change document: "only surfaces from that landing").
##      Any other command lets the clock expire: the slide completes, Gentry's
##      landing block runs unchanged, and she is standing in the Fruit Stall.
##
## Not built yet, by design — these land with the chase + dress increment
## (TE20, the satchel, the boots): the capture on the ride-out landing, the
## one-turn change window behind the stall, the one-turn return window, the
## boots identification at the Fruit Stall, and the mercenaries moving to the
## stall when she lingers. Until then the sweep model alone reads her moves.
##
## THE FOUR CABLES (2026-08-30): the source's four named cables and its
## landing table (`story.ni:3728-3830`) are carried — northeast: Fruit Stall
## (the designed two-turn path above), southeast: Hat Stall, southwest:
## Exotic Gems Stall, northwest: Northwest Junction. The other three are
## single-turn slides running Gentry's whole block and landing her, as the
## source does — no death on the landing. What follows is the source's too
## (David, 2026-08-30: "whatever the original source does and if there's a
## gap, then I want to know"): during `chase` the landing opens the source's
## `Market Escape` (`story.ni:4132-4200`, built in `mercenaries.chord`) —
## the six-row spool of closing shouts, capture on the sixth; during
## `hunted` the sweep simply resumes. A bare `slide down cables` takes the
## SOUTHEAST cable, as the source's "does the player mean" default does
## (`story.ni:3756-3758`) — RULED (David, 2026-08-30): "if the default was SE
## in the original source, it stays that way." The designed path must be
## named: `slide down northeast cable`.
##
## THE GAP, RULED (David, 2026-08-30): "the other three scenarios are
## intentional — only the one wire is the escape route." In the source every
## landing was survivable (the silk cloak went on anywhere); in the remake
## the change happens only Behind Fruit Stall and the east exit is watched
## while she is the urchin, so from the three seen landings the spool can
## only end in capture — by design. The ride-out landing (front of the
## Fruit Stall) is the ruled instant death and opens no spool. Recorded in
## the change document ("The other three cables").
##
## The source's `slide down <cable> with <garment>` and `hang/loop/wrap
## <garment> on <cable>` phrasings — the walkthrough's own (`story.ni:12419`)
## — are the same action with an instrument slot. Only the gray cloak is
## slide-worthy in Chapter 1 (the belt, rope, reins and harness are later
## chapters' objects), so the barehanded gate is the only check: an
## instrument that is not the cloak is ignored and the slide runs on the
## cloak. Gentry's "You can't loop [that] around the cable" waits on a slot
## test in a must-line, which Chord does not have (noted seam).
## The instrument-FIRST shapes (`hang/loop/wrap the item … the target`,
## `put the item over the target`) reach the cable since Sharpee 5.3.0 (GH
## #333, fixed 2026-09-03): the parser used to seat slots by position, so the
## target at position 1 landed in the indirect-object seat and the direct
## object stayed empty. No story change was needed; `hang cloak on northeast
## cable` is pinned in the tree beside the `slide down` phrasings.
##
## Sliding while held refuses with the grab line (`merc-held`), mirroring
## the player's `on going` refusal — found 2026-08-30 when a held Jack slid
## away on the hunted probe; the source's chase never lets them up the post,
## but the port's sweep can reach the top.
##
## Not carried: the per-cable description's "attached to the ground at [the
## landing, once visited]" tail — Chord has no visited condition, so every
## cable names its corner of the market; and `untie cable` — no stdlib
## untying. `cut`, `climb`, `push`/`pull` refuse with Gentry's lines (the
## `anchor-cable` trait; the southeast cable carries its own push/pull so the
## monkey's chatter can ride on them — one interceptor per (entity, action),
## GH #332). The four diagonal exits at the Top of the Post refuse with the
## barehanded line, as the source's `Before going` rule does.
##
## Also held: `hang <garment> on cable` with the other slide-worthy garments
## (none exist yet).
##
## Structural choices for David to rename or redirect at will: the mid-slide
## room's name ("On the Wire") and Behind Fruit Stall's return exit direction
## (southwest). Every finished line is Gentry's; the marked TODO texts
## are David's to write.
##
## Known seam: `move the player to <room>` narrates nothing, so a landing
## prints only its authored line, not the destination's description (`look`
## still answers). The reveal is therefore emitted as its own phrase on the
## slide turn. Whether the landings should also re-describe is polish for the
## change-window increment.
## ===========================================================================

define timer sliding for the cables
end timer

## The ride-out clock expires after exactly one mid-slide command — Gentry's
## landing block (`story.ni:3822`) runs unchanged; the change document's
## caught-on-landing consequence waits on the chase increment.

create the cables
  aka cable, anchor cables, anchor cable, wires, wire, braided wire
  scenery, plural
  in the Top of the Post
  slides-to-hat-stall
  anchor-cable
  taut-cable

  There are four cables, each made of thick, braided wire. They stretch
  southwest, southeast, northeast, and northwest to the four corners of the
  market.

  when sliding expires, while the player is in On the Wire
    phrase slide-ground
    phrase slide-spotted when chase
    phrase slide-landing
    move the old gray cloak to the Fruit Stall
    move the player to the Fruit Stall
    kill the player ride-out-caught
  end when

## The four named cables (`story.ni:3728-3760`). Each description is Gentry's
## cable text with the corner tail (see the header on the visited tail). The
## northeast cable carries the designed two-turn machine (`slideable`); the
## other three run the source's single-turn slide to their landing, and the
## collective `cables` — a bare `slide down cables` — takes the southeast
## one, the source's default (David's ruling, header). The `sliding` clock
## stays declared on the collective; only its expiry clause lives here.

create the northeast cable
  aka ne cable, northeast wire, northeast anchor cable
  scenery
  in the Top of the Post
  slideable
  anchor-cable

  The cable is made of thick, braided wire. One end is attached to the top
  of the pole that you are currently balancing on; the other end is attached
  to the ground at the northeast corner of the market, far below.

create the southeast cable
  aka se cable, southeast wire, southeast anchor cable
  scenery
  in the Top of the Post
  anchor-cable
  slides-to-hat-stall

  The cable is made of thick, braided wire. One end is attached to the top
  of the pole that you are currently balancing on; the other end is attached
  to the ground at the southeast corner of the market, far below.

  phrase detail while the monkey is here:
    The monkey balances easily on the cable, about 3 feet out from the top of
    the post.

  on the player pulling
    refuse when the monkey is not here: cable-taut
    refuse cable-taut-monkey
  end on

  on the player pushing
    refuse when the monkey is not here: cable-taut
    refuse cable-taut-monkey
  end on

create the southwest cable
  aka sw cable, southwest wire, southwest anchor cable
  scenery
  in the Top of the Post
  anchor-cable
  slides-to-gems-stall
  taut-cable

  The cable is made of thick, braided wire. One end is attached to the top
  of the pole that you are currently balancing on; the other end is attached
  to the ground at the southwest corner of the market, far below.

create the northwest cable
  aka nw cable, northwest wire, northwest anchor cable
  scenery
  in the Top of the Post
  anchor-cable
  slides-to-junction
  taut-cable

  The cable is made of thick, braided wire. One end is attached to the top
  of the pole that you are currently balancing on; the other end is attached
  to the ground at the northwest corner of the market, far below.

## The three single-turn slides (`story.ni:3822`): one trait per landing,
## because a dispatch action's clause lives in a trait and a `move`'s place
## is a static name — flat and greppable, the peering precedent.

define trait slides-to-hat-stall
  on the player sliding
    refuse when the wandering mercenaries is aggressive and the wandering mercenaries is here: merc-held
    the player must have the old gray cloak: slide-barehanded
    phrase slide-first-half
    phrase slide-monkey-leaps when the monkey is here
    phrase slide-wind
    phrase slide-ground
    phrase slide-spotted when chase
    phrase slide-landing
    reset the player's waiting
    reset the player's pole-escape
    reset the player's pole-problem
    reset the player's pole-destruction
    move the old gray cloak to the Hat Stall
    move the player to the Hat Stall
    phrase escape-shouting when chase
    start the player's market-escape when chase
  end on
end trait

define trait slides-to-gems-stall
  on the player sliding
    refuse when the wandering mercenaries is aggressive and the wandering mercenaries is here: merc-held
    the player must have the old gray cloak: slide-barehanded
    phrase slide-first-half
    phrase slide-wind
    phrase slide-ground
    phrase slide-spotted when chase
    phrase slide-landing
    reset the player's waiting
    reset the player's pole-escape
    reset the player's pole-problem
    reset the player's pole-destruction
    move the old gray cloak to the Exotic Gems Stall
    move the player to the Exotic Gems Stall
    phrase escape-shouting when chase
    start the player's market-escape when chase
  end on
end trait

define trait slides-to-junction
  on the player sliding
    refuse when the wandering mercenaries is aggressive and the wandering mercenaries is here: merc-held
    the player must have the old gray cloak: slide-barehanded
    phrase slide-first-half
    phrase slide-wind
    phrase slide-ground
    phrase slide-spotted when chase
    phrase slide-landing
    reset the player's waiting
    reset the player's pole-escape
    reset the player's pole-problem
    reset the player's pole-destruction
    move the old gray cloak to the Northwest Junction
    move the player to the Northwest Junction
    phrase escape-shouting when chase
    start the player's market-escape when chase
  end on
end trait

## The cables' refusals (`story.ni:3763-3790`): climbing a cable is the
## source's sliding-barehanded; cutting and interfering have their own lines.
## `taut-cable` is the push/pull pair for the cables the monkey never sits on.

define trait anchor-cable
  on the player climbing
    refuse slide-barehanded
  end on

  on the player cutting
    refuse cable-too-tough
  end on
end trait

define trait taut-cable
  on the player pulling
    refuse cable-taut
  end on

  on the player pushing
    refuse cable-taut
  end on
end trait

