## killed"). Keyed rather than inline: an inline `kill` body is named by its
## fragment-relative line and column, and two fragments can collide (GH
## #324).

define phrase ride-out-caught
  (TODO during play-testing — Caught on the ride-out landing and killed — a
  hard ending.)
end phrase

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE SLIDE — first turn
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define action sliding
  grammar
    slide the target
    slide down the target
    slide along the target
    slide the target with or using the item
    slide down the target with or using the item
    slide along the target with or using the item
    hang the item on or over or around the target
    loop the item on or over or around the target
    wrap the item on or over or around the target
    put the item over or around the target
  the item is an instrument

define trait slideable
  on the player sliding
    refuse when the wandering mercenaries is aggressive and the wandering mercenaries is here: merc-held
    the player must have the old gray cloak: slide-barehanded
    phrase slide-first-half
    reset the player's waiting
    reset the player's pole-escape
    reset the player's pole-problem
    reset the player's pole-destruction
    move the player to On the Wire
    phrase on-the-wire-reveal
    start the cables' sliding
  end on
end trait

## The mid-slide texts are David's — RULED (2026-08-24), with the design
## stated: "learn by dying." The reveal is the extended wind sentence at the
## end of the slide turn (`on-the-wire-reveal` below — Gentry's arms now end
## before the wind sentence so it prints once); nothing surfaces `let go` as
## the move, and the ride-out is fatal. A mid-air `look` answers with the
## terse room body below.

create On the Wire
  a room

  The wire (sliding towards the Fruit Stall).

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## LETTING GO — the hidden landing
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define action letting-go
  grammar
    let go
  refuse when the player is not in On the Wire: let-go-nothing
  phrase let-go-drop
  move the old gray cloak to Behind Fruit Stall
  move the player to Behind Fruit Stall
  reset the cables' sliding
  start the player's lingering

## Bare `drop` is the mid-slide synonym (David, 2026-08-24) — its own action
## so its elsewhere-refusal can differ from LET GO's. Outside the wire it
## prints a static stand-in for the stdlib missing-noun prompt: the real
## routing (story grammar losing to the stdlib parse outside its room, and
## the next-command answer flow) is platform work — GH #317 and #318.

define action releasing
  grammar
    drop
  refuse when the player is not in On the Wire: drop-what
  phrase let-go-drop
  move the old gray cloak to Behind Fruit Stall
  move the player to Behind Fruit Stall
  reset the cables' sliding
  start the player's lingering

define phrase drop-what
  What do you want to drop?
end phrase

## The two windows (change document, "Both timing questions"): a second turn
## back here expires the player's `lingering` clock, moves the pair to the
## Fruit Stall and marks this room `watched`; stepping out then is death
## (ruled 2026-08-24: "she's caught and killed"). The death is an `after
## going` REACTION, not an `on going` intercept: a `kill` inside an intercept
## does not stop the move (the room description, the dash-away line and a
## platform warning all printed before the engine stopped — 2026-08-29), and
## the pair's posture is left alone so Jack's own `on going … aggressive`
## refusal cannot swallow the step. The clocks are declared in
## `disguise.chord`.

create Behind Fruit Stall
  a room
  aka behind the fruit stall
  states: hidden, watched
  southwest to the Fruit Stall, one-way

  Cramped and flea-ridden with barely room to move. The market is out to the
  southwest.

  when the player's lingering expires, while the player is in Behind Fruit Stall
    move the wandering mercenaries to the Fruit Stall
    change Behind Fruit Stall to watched
  end when

  after the player going while Behind Fruit Stall is watched
    kill the player stepped-out-caught
  end after

define phrase stepped-out-caught
  (TODO during play-testing — She steps out into the mercenaries waiting at
  the Fruit Stall: caught and killed, no free turn.)
end phrase

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE LINES — Gentry's, split across the two turns
##
## The source's single slide block (`story.ni:3822`) splits at the wind
## sentence: the wrap-and-kick-off half is turn one, the ground-rushes-up
## half is the ride-out. The heartbeat sentences are the source's own
## `[first time]` insert, carried as the first-time arm.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define phrase slide-first-half, first-time
  You wrap one end of your cloak around your hand, then squat down and sling
  the other end around the anchor cable. You give it a good, hard yank to
  test your grip. You take a deep breath. You try to convince your heartbeat
  to slow down, or at least continue its dreadful hammering in some part of
  your body other than your throat. You fail. Then, finally, you swing down
  under the wire and kick off into space.
or
  You wrap one end of your cloak around your hand, then squat down and sling
  the other end around the anchor cable. You give it a good, hard yank to
  test your grip. Then you swing down under the wire and kick off into space.
end phrase

## The landing half is three phrases so the source's own inserts keep their
## places: the `[if final chase is happening]` shout sentence sits between
## the harness holding and the feet hitting dirt, and the southeast cable's
## monkey leap sits before the wind sentence.

define phrase slide-wind
  The wind is exhilarating as you zip down the line.
end phrase

define phrase slide-ground
  The ground rushes up at you with terrifying speed. Your cloak whizzes
  hotly against the rough wire, and you vaguely smell smoke, but your
  impromptu harness holds.
end phrase

define phrase slide-spotted
  Shouts ring out and fingers point as the mercenaries spot you and race to
  where they think you will land.
end phrase

define phrase slide-landing
  Suddenly your feet hit dirt. You let go of the cloak and tumble to the
  hard ground, knocking over a couple of shoppers before you roll to a stop.
  The next moment you are back on your feet, bruised and breathless.
end phrase

define phrase slide-monkey-leaps
  The monkey sees you coming and leaps over you, landing deftly on the top
  of the post. Its angry shrieks fade quickly as you pick up speed.
end phrase

define phrase cable-too-tough
  The cables are far too thick and tough for you to cut.
end phrase

define phrase cable-taut
  The cables are so thick and so taut, they don't even budge.
end phrase

define phrase cable-taut-monkey
  The cables are so thick and so taut, they don't even budge. The monkey
  chatters happily at your fruitless efforts to dislodge it.
end phrase

define phrase slide-barehanded
  The cable is too steep for you to climb down, and the rough wire would tear
  up your hands. But if you had something you could loop over the cable, you
  might be able to slide down while hanging underneath.
end phrase

define phrase on-the-wire-reveal
  The wind is exhilarating as you zip down the line...coming up behind the
  fruit stall...
end phrase

define phrase let-go-drop
  You release one side of the cloak, dropping hard to a small space behind
  the fruit stall.
end phrase

## `let go` anywhere but mid-slide — David's line (2026-08-24).

define phrase let-go-nothing
  You meditate for a second, just like they showed you in Maiden House.
end phrase
